What if code isn’t just a tool, but a whole new way to store and verify knowledge?

This paper argues code is the *first* medium in history that can check its own facts—automatically, every time it runs. Out of 1.4M lines from LLM coding agents, 80% were “knowledge-as-code” and 28% of all persisted code lines came from live runtime observations (“learning by execution”). Tests wrapped around new facts, and months later, 72% of learned facts still held true.

From self-healing infra to executable science papers, the future may be “science as code”—where claims, evidence and checks live as one artifact.

